    "lawCode": "Amd §§13.37-a & 13.38, Ment Hyg L",
    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S1696                Revised 03/09/15\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to transitional\ncare\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nTo preserve the majority of due process protections for people with\ndevelopmental disabilities as provided by chapter 478 of the laws of\n2014.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThe bill would amend mental hygiene law by:\n\n*Preserving the newly created requirement that notice be given in\nwriting, however, certified mail transmittal is not required.\n\n*Preserving the requirement that another person, other than the\ndevelopmentally disabled person receiving transitional care, be\nprovided written notice of the Office for People with Developmental\nDisabilities' (OPWDD) intended change in placement, however, a third\nperson need not be notified.\n\n*Preserving the requirement that interested parties be given adequate\nadvance written notice in advance of the date of OPWDD's intended\nchange of placement, and during such notice period, interested parties\ncan initiate an administrative appeal, however, the minimum advance\nnotice is 30 days rather than 45 days.\n\n*Preserving the requirements that OPWDD's determination be provided in\nwriting to an objecting party; that such determination shall be the\nfinal administrative remedy provided by OPWDD; and that such\ndetermination shall be subject to further appeal through judicial\nreview pursuant to Article 78 of the Civil Practice Laws and Rules.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2014, S.7374 was chaptered (478), providing much needed due process\nprotections for developmentally disabled persons who \"age out\" of\nspecial education funding at their current residential facilities and\nobject to the particular appropriateness of OPWDD's proposed transfer\nand placement.\n\nDue to the fiscal impact and constraints on the State's ability to\nexpeditiously transition individuals to other facilities, the\nExecutive and Legislature agreed to enact a chapter amendment. The\nresult of such an agreement has been the preservation of many of the\ndue process protections established by chapter 478 of the laws of\n2014.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nUndetermined.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall be deemed to have\nbeen in full force and effect on the same date as chapter 478 of the\nlaws of 2014 took effect.\n\n",
